<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="ch9-section-isobaric-coordinates-subsection-mass-conservation" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Mass conservation</title>

    <p>
        Returning to the basic statement of mass conservation for an air parcel from
        <xref ref="ch6-section-lagrangian" text="custom">Chapter 6</xref>:
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-mass-conservation-reproduced" number="yes">
            \frac{Dm}{D t} = 0
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        Recall this statement means the air parcel of interest does not exchange mass with its environment.
    </p>

    <p>
        The air parcel's mass is given by
    </p>
    
    <p>
        <md number="yes">
            m = \rho V
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        where <m>\rho</m> is the air parcel's density and <m>V</m> is its volume. 
        As as we have done since <xref ref="chapter-3" text="type-global" />,
        we assume the air parcel is a rectangular prism with length <m>\delta x</m>, width <m>\delta y</m>, and height <m>\delta z</m>,
        so its volume is
    </p>
        
    <p>
        <md number="yes">
            V = \delta x \, \delta y \, \delta z
        </md>
    </p>
    
    <p>
        But now that we are using <m>p</m> rather than <m>z</m> as the vertical coordinate and have the hydrostatic approximation at our disposal,
        we can rearrange the mathematical equation of hydrostatic balance to solve for <m>\delta z</m>
    </p>

    <p>
        <md number="yes">
            \delta z= - \frac{\delta p}{\rho g}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        and then we can substitute this expression into <m>V</m>:
    </p>

    <p>
        <md number="yes">
            V
            =
            \delta x \, \delta y \, \delta z
            =
            \delta x \, \delta y \left( -\frac{\delta p}{\rho g} \right)
            =
            -\frac{\delta x \, \delta y \, \delta p}{\rho g}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        Then
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-mass-isobaric" number="yes">
            m
            =
            \rho V
            =
            \rho \left( -\frac{\delta x \, \delta y \, \delta p}{\rho g} \right)
            =
            -\frac{\delta x \, \delta y \, \delta p}{g}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        If we neglect variations in <m>g</m> by assuming changes in <m>z</m> are small,
        we can treat <m>g</m> as a constant and write the statement of mass conservation for an air parcel as
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-mass-conservation-isobaric-start" number="yes">
            \frac{Dm}{Dt}
            =
            -\frac{1}{g}\frac{D \left( \delta x \, \delta y \, \delta p \right)}{Dt}
            =
            0
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        We expand <xref ref="eqn-mass-conservation-isobaric-start" text="type-global" /> by applying the product rule:
    </p>

    <p>
        <md>
            <mrow>
                \frac{Dm}{Dt}
                \amp =
                -\frac{1}{g}\frac{D \left( \delta x \, \delta y \, \delta p \right)}{Dt}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
                \amp =
                -\frac{1}{g}
                \left[
                    \delta y \, \delta p \frac{D \delta x}{Dt}
                    + 
                    \delta x \, \delta p \frac{D \delta y}{Dt}
                    +
                    \delta x \, \delta y \frac{D \delta p}{Dt}
                \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow number="yes">
                \amp = 0
            </mrow>
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        Since <m>\dfrac{D \delta x}{Dt} = \delta u</m>,
        <m>\dfrac{D \delta y}{Dt} = \delta v</m>,
        and <m>\dfrac{D \delta p}{Dt} = \delta \omega</m>,
        we have
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-mass-conservation-isobaric-intermediate" number="yes">
            \frac{Dm}{Dt}
            =
            -\frac{1}{g}
            \left[
                \delta y \, \delta p \, \delta u
                +
                \delta x \, \delta p \, \delta v
                +
                \delta x \, \delta y \, \delta \omega
            \right]
            = 0
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        Dividing <xref ref="eqn-mass-conservation-isobaric-intermediate" text="type-global" />
        by <m>m = -\dfrac{\delta x \, \delta y \, \delta p}{g}</m>
        as given in <xref ref="eqn-mass-isobaric" text="type-global" />
        produces
    </p>

    <p>
        <md>
            <mrow>
                \frac{1}{m} \frac{Dm}{Dt}
                \amp =
                \frac{1}{m} \cdot
                -\frac{1}{g}
                \left[
                    \delta y \, \delta p \, \delta u
                    +
                    \delta x \, \delta p \, \delta v
                    +
                    \delta x \, \delta y \, \delta \omega
                \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
                \amp =
                -\frac{g}{\delta x \, \delta y \, \delta p} \cdot
                -\frac{1}{g}
                \left[
                    \delta y \, \delta p \, \delta u
                    +
                    \delta x \, \delta p \, \delta v
                    +
                    \delta x \, \delta y \, \delta \omega
                \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
                \amp =
                \frac{1}{\delta x \, \delta y \, \delta p}
                \left[
                    \delta y \, \delta p \, \delta u
                    +
                    \delta x \, \delta p \, \delta v
                    +
                    \delta x \, \delta y \, \delta \omega
                \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
                \amp =
                \frac{\delta u}{\delta x}
                +
                \frac{\delta v}{\delta y}
                +
                \frac{\delta \omega}{\delta p}
            </mrow>
            <mrow xml:id="eqn-mass-conservation-isobaric-deltas" number="yes">
                \amp = 0
            </mrow>
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        As <m>\delta x \to 0</m>, <m>\delta y \to 0</m>, and <m>\delta p \to 0</m>
        (which is an acceptable assumption for an immeasurably small air parcel),
        <xref ref="eqn-mass-conservation-isobaric-deltas" text="type-global" /> becomes
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-mass-conservation-isobaric" number="yes">
            \frac{\partial u}{\partial x} +
            \frac{\partial v}{\partial y} +
            \frac{\partial \omega}{\partial p}
            = 0
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        which can be written as
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-mass-conservation-isobaric-subscript" number="yes">
            \left(
                \frac{\partial u}{\partial x} +
                \frac{\partial v}{\partial y}
            \right)_p
            +
            \frac{\partial \omega}{\partial p}
            = 0
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        where the subscript "p" emphasizes that the horizontal derivatives are taken on an isobaric surface.
    </p>

    <p>
        <xref ref="eqn-mass-conservation-isobaric" text="type-global" />
        and <xref ref="eqn-mass-conservation-isobaric-subscript" text="type-global" />
        further can be written as
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-mass-conservation-isobaric-vector" number="yes">
            \nabla_p \cdot \vec{v}
            =
            \delta_p
            =
            -\frac{\partial \omega}{\partial p}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        where <m>\vec{v} = u \, \hat{i} + v \, \hat{j}</m> is the horizontal wind and
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-horizontal-divergence-isobaric" number="yes">
            \delta_p
            =
            \left(
                \frac{\partial u}{\partial x} + \frac{\partial v}{\partial y}
            \right)_p
            =
            \nabla_p \cdot \vec{v}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        is the <term>horizontal divergence on an isobaric surface</term>.
    </p>

    <p>
        Note that <xref ref="eqn-mass-conservation-isobaric" text="type-global" />
        looks like the incompressible form of mass conservation in height coordinates,
        <xref ref="eqn-incompessibility-scalar" text="type-global" />
        of <xref ref="ch6-section-incompressibility" text="custom">Chapter 6</xref>,
        and <xref ref="eqn-mass-conservation-isobaric-vector" text="type-global" />
        looks like its rearrangement in terms of horizontal divergence<m>\delta</m> in height coordinates,
        <xref ref="eqn-incompressibility-vertical-wind" text="type-global" />
        of <xref ref="ch6-section-incompressibility" text="custom">Chapter 6</xref>.
        But in making <xref ref="eqn-mass-conservation-isobaric" text="type-global" />
        and <xref ref="eqn-mass-conservation-isobaric-vector" text="type-global" />,
        we did not assume the air parcel is incompressible!
        Yet <xref ref="eqn-mass-conservation-isobaric" text="type-global" />
        and <xref ref="eqn-mass-conservation-isobaric-vector" text="type-global" />
        still enable us to relate the divergence of the horizontal wind to the vertical wind
        (in this case given by <m>\omega</m>, not <m>w</m>).
        This simplification of the mass continuity equation without assuming incompressibility is a major benefit of using isobaric coordinates.
    </p>

</subsection>